<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eloy Alfaro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eloy Alfaro was the leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1895">Liberal Revolution of 1895</a> and president from 1895 to 1901 and from 1906 to 1911. He separated church and state, introduced civil marriage and secular education, and completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> railway in 1908. In January 1912 he was imprisoned in Quito, where a mob killed him and burned his body.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro.jpg" alt="Eloy Alfaro" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eloy_Alfaro2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Eloy Alfaro Delgado was born in Montecristi, Manabí, on 25 June 1842. His father, Manuel Alfaro y González, was a Spanish Republican who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> as a political exile. Alfaro aligned himself in youth with anticlerical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and fought against Presidents García Moreno, Borrero, Veintemilla and Camaño, which earned him the name Viejo Luchador, the Old Warrior.</p><p>He spent a fortune acquired with the help of his Panamanian wife, Ana Paredes Arosemena, on these campaigns. He nearly drowned in the naval battle of Alajuela, saving himself by clinging to a barrel when his ship sank, and fought at Montecristi, San Mateo, Esmeraldas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, Cuenca and elsewhere. In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> he was given the rank of General de División by the Nicaraguan Congress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1895, as head of the Radical Liberals, Alfaro deposed President Vicente Lucio Salazar and declared himself dictator on 5 June. He was named constitutional president from 17 January 1897 until 1 September 1901. The chief accomplishment of this government was the principle of secularism, and French architects were commissioned for public buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>.</p><p>In 1906 he led another revolt, deposing the elected President Lizardo García, and ruled as supreme dictator until 12 August 1911. He enacted freedom of speech, civil marriage and divorce, built public schools and introduced free secular education. He seized much Church property, expelled religious orders and barred new monasteries and convents, against the opposition of the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, Federico González Suárez. His chief public work was the Trans-Andean Railroad connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> to Quito.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His former supporters removed him from office in 1911. After an attempt to return to power he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, came back on 4 January 1912, attempted another coup and was arrested by General Leonidas Plaza. On 28 January 1912 pro-Catholic soldiers shouting Muerte al indio Alfaro, backed by a mob, broke into his prison, dragged him and his colleagues through the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and burnt the bodies at El Ejido.</p><p>His remains were buried in secret, later taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, and in part re-interred in Montecristi on the initiative of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-correa">Rafael Correa</a>. His effigy appeared on the 50-cent coin issued in 2000, and the Ecuadorian Army's military college bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eloy Alfaro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Eloy Alfaro</b>’s Liberal Revolution of 1895 was likewise won in campaigns from the coast into the Sierra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Monte Cristi, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-1.05,-80.67&amp;year=1887" aria-label="Monte Cristi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2309 2203 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eloy Alfaro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2759 2473h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2759 2473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-1.05,-80.67&amp;year=1887">What was here in 1887? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
